nd life to the self  inside that felt trapped beneath you  know the ideals and expectations  that we have for women and i think i  started um recovering that  self in sobriety you know i think it  started for me  when i got sober at 25 years old and i  sat down next to a woman at a recovery  meeting and i said life is so hard and i  feel like  everyone else has some sort of secret to  being human that i don't have  because it feels so much harder for me  than it looks for everyone else  and she said the secret of life  is not that it feels hard for you  because you're doing it wrong it feels  hard because you're finally doing it  right  right that that being human is hard  and and and sitting with uncomfortable  feelings and not numbing them is hard  that's one of the things in your book  that means so much to me is  you're speaking of and if you're in  cognitive therapy or  you know anyone who embraces embracing  the pain  and the discomfort because we are all  doing everything whether it's on our  phone  or having a drink or eating food or  talking even to someone just about it  but not really  like you know getting to the bottom of  it we are all  desperate to avoid the pain and  discomfort and the way  that you help us get excited about it  is so actionable and you've gotten sober  you've found a whole new life path  i find that if you are talking about big  ticket items  this is the book for you if you are  talking about small  changes this is the book for you if you  are talking about how to get  in touch with your truth and your inner  voice this is the book for you  i mean you are setting people free and i  can't thank you  enough for my experience  with this book and it's so personal and  by the way you are the best  funniest wittiest writer ever and i'm  not you deserve  awards everywhere for your style of  writing and your wording  it has poetry it's sexy it's liberating  you  are just my hero and i can't believe i  actually got to talk to you and i'm all